
Stop holding the deck twice, and say which allocation is about to fail.

A deck that dies in the kernel OOM killer never reaches u_calloc's error
path, so ccx says nothing about what it was allocating.  CCX_ALLOC_TRACE=<MB>
logs every allocation at or above that size, with the source line, before it
is attempted (u_calloc.c, u_realloc.c).

inpc holds the deck as text padded to 132 bytes a line: readinput.c grows it
1.1x and then RENEWs to 132*nline, reserving the maximum line length for every
line.  Every reader walks the ipoinpc offset table, so sizing to
ipoinpc[*nline] holds the same deck in less space:

    L_mesh 0.0060, 1.7 GB deck    after growth    after RENEW
      before                      1.734 GB        3.110 GB
      after                       1.734 GB        1.592 GB

VERIFIED answer-neutral on the acceptance deck (F_x = 1.552992E+07 in double,
every digit).  The trace is off unless the variable is set.


--- readinput.c
+++ readinput.c
@@ -477,7 +477,15 @@
   }while(1);
 
   inp[3*ipoinp[2*ikey-1]-2]=*nline;
-  RENEW(inpc,char,(long long)132**nline);
+  /* F-barES-FEM-T4: size inpc to what was actually read, not to 132 bytes per line.
+     Every access goes through the ipoinpc offset table -- getnewline.f walks
+     ipoinpc(iline-1)+1 .. ipoinpc(iline) and nothing strides inpc at a fixed
+     132 -- so ipoinpc[*nline] is exactly the number of characters stored.
+     Padding every line to the maximum costs, on the 0.0060 deck,
+     3.110 GB against ~1.7 GB of content, and inpc lives until the very end
+     of the run (it is freed with ipoinp/inp only after the last step), so
+     that slack is held straight through the factorisation. */
+  RENEW(inpc,char,ipoinpc[*nline]>0?ipoinpc[*nline]:1);
   
   *inp_size=3*ipoinp[2*ikey-1];
   RENEW(inp,ITG,*inp_size);
